---
/**
 * MEMOIR / MONSOON (spec §4.5). The shader renders the drought-state dither
 * breaking into falling glyph rain with lightning glitches, resolving to a
 * PNW palette — driven live by the 'iw:weather' event this component wires
 * up via src/scripts/weather.ts. The DOM layer is the memoir copy scaffold.
 */
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import type { SectionSpec } from '../../design/tokens';

interface Props {
  spec: SectionSpec;
}

const { spec } = Astro.props;
---

<Section spec={spec}>
  <h2 data-egg-anchor="monsoon-cloud">Monsoon</h2>

  <p class="scaffold">
    Phoenix teaches drought as the default sky. The monsoon arrives as an
    event: the dust wall first, then lightning, then rain that the ground
    swallows in minutes. Farther north the rain is a climate, and the green
    there is permanent.
  </p>

  <p class="scaffold">
    I was born a fish. Full moon, the Fish on one side of the sky and the
    Maiden on the other, and the first thing the world did was put me in the
    desert. Phoenix as a boy, where the sky is not your friend and not your
    enemy, just absent, and you build a whole life around the absence. Then a
    valley up the Gila for my teens, a river with a name and mostly no water
    in it, settled by my own blood five generations back. A fish raised in a
    dry riverbed and taught to call it home. Shade is a strategy. Water is a
    decision. You do not count on anything coming from above.
  </p>
  <p class="scaffold">
    Then every summer, for a few weeks, the sky remembers it exists. The dust
    wall comes first, a mile high, then the lightning, then rain so hard the
    ground can't take it and the streets run brown. Violent, brief, gone by
    morning, and you stand in the steam and understand that you just watched
    the whole year's grace arrive in forty minutes. That's how I used to
    love. That's how I used to work. That's how she used to reach me.
    Drought, drought, drought, flood.
  </p>
  <p class="scaffold">
    A fish can live in that. It can't thrive in it. I'm done building for
    absence. The move north is the plain version of a thing I've said six
    ways on this page already: I want to live where the rain is a climate and
    not an event. Where green isn't a miracle. Where you don't have to earn
    the water, and the thing that comes down on you is something you didn't
    make. The desert taught me to carry weight, to run, and to turn around
    for proof. Water teaches the only lesson left, which is to be still
    inside it. Cosmic consideration. Divine patience. Faith in the path. The
    fish goes home: not back down the dry bed, but north, under a sky that
    rains as a habit, into water deep enough that it can stop swimming and
    still be carried.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    This section is wired to the actual sky over Phoenix. When it rains
    there, it rains here. I wanted the site to be honest about where it came
    from.
  </p>
</Section>

<script>
  import { initWeather } from '../../scripts/weather';
  initWeather();
</script>

<style>
  .scaffold {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .note {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
</style>
